<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="a326ea07">&lt;p&gt;
    Test effort estimation is the process of determining approximation time and cost to complete a defined task. Accurate
    estimates often result in the efficient use of resources and avoid delay in the project delivery. Calculation of the
    test estimation is typically based on the following:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Past experience of the participating members
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Assumptions
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Available data from previous projects
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;
    Two commonly used approaches for the estimation of test effort are given below:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;div&gt;
            Top down estimation. This approach uses the actual estimates from previous similar projects as a basis for
            estimation. Following techniques fall under top-down approach:
        &lt;/div&gt;
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                &lt;div&gt;
                    Functional point analysis
                &lt;/div&gt;
            &lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                &lt;div&gt;
                    Test point analysis
                &lt;/div&gt;
            &lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;div&gt;
            Bottom up estimation. This approach uses work breakdown structure (WBS) for estimation. Estimates are created
            for all tasks at the lowest level of the WBS and then these are accumulated to determine the estimates for the
            whole project. Following techniques belong to bottom-up approach:
        &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li style=&quot;LIST-STYLE-TYPE: none&quot;&gt;
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                &lt;div&gt;
                    Work break down structure
                &lt;/div&gt;
            &lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                &lt;div&gt;
                    Wide Band Delphi
                &lt;/div&gt;
            &lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;
    Bottom up approach is considered more accurate but it takes longer time, whereas top-down approach is fast but often
    considered less accurate.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
